course: biology
lesson: 353
title: Skin: layers, sweat, goosebumps, freckles and sunburn
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Your skin is an organ, like your heart or lungs, and it is the biggest one you have: a waterproof, germ-proof, self-repairing coat that also helps keep your temperature right.
explain: It has three main layers: the thin outer epidermis that protects, the dermis below with blood vessels, nerves, sweat glands and hair roots, and a fat layer underneath that cushions and keeps you warm.
explain: Look at a sunbeam in your bedroom: those floating specks of dust are partly your own dead skin, because the outer layer is always flaking off and being replaced from below.
explain: Skin also reacts to the world: it sweats to cool you, makes goosebumps when you are cold, makes extra brown melanin in the sun, and burns if the sun's ultraviolet light is too strong.
fact: Skin is the body's largest organ; an adult's skin would cover roughly two square metres if laid out flat.
fact: Skin keeps water in and germs out and helps keep your temperature right; it is thinnest on the eyelids and thickest on the soles of the feet.
fact: Skin has three main layers: the outer epidermis, the dermis with blood vessels and nerves, and a fat layer underneath; the outer layer has no blood vessels, so a very light scrape does not bleed.
fact: People shed tens of thousands of tiny dead skin flakes every minute, which become part of house dust, and tiny dust mites eat them.
fact: The outer layer of skin replaces itself about every four weeks, bit by bit, while a snake sheds its whole skin in one go.
fact: Skin has two to four million sweat glands; sweat cools you as it dries, because drying takes heat from the skin, and on a humid day it dries slowly and cools less.
fact: Dogs sweat only a little, through their paws, and cool down mainly by panting; pigs barely sweat at all, so they roll in mud to keep cool.
fact: Each hair has a tiny muscle that pulls it upright, making a goosebump; in furry animals raised fur traps warm air or makes them look bigger, like a scared cat.
fact: Some people get goosebumps from music they love, and scientists are still studying why.
fact: Skin colour depends on how much melanin, a brown colour, the skin makes; everyone has roughly the same number of colour-making cells, they just make different amounts.
fact: Freckles are spots of extra melanin; sun makes them darker, they often fade in winter, and people with red hair and fair skin often have more.
fact: Ultraviolet light from the sun damages skin cells and causes sunburn; you can burn on a cloudy day, snow and water reflect sunlight onto you, and sunburn in childhood raises the risk of skin cancer later.
fact: Skin makes vitamin D in sunlight, which helps bones use calcium; in Britain the winter sun is too weak for this, so many people take vitamin D in autumn and winter.
exercise: What is the largest organ of the body? | The skin | The liver | The brain | The lungs
exercise: Where is your skin thinnest? | On the eyelids | On the soles of the feet | On the back | On the knees
exercise: Which is the outer layer of the skin? | The epidermis | The dermis | The fat layer | The muscle layer
exercise: Which layer of skin holds the blood vessels and nerves? | The dermis | The epidermis | The nail layer | The hair layer
exercise: Why does a very light scrape not bleed? | The outer layer has no blood vessels | The blood is too thick and sticky to come out of such a small hole | The skin closes in a split second | Scrapes only hurt the hair
exercise: What is much of the dust in a bedroom made of? | Tiny flakes of dead skin | Bits of the ceiling paint | Dried spit from talking | Pollen only
exercise: About how often does the outer layer of skin replace itself? | About every four weeks | Every single day | Once a year on your birthday | Never, you keep the same skin
exercise: How does sweat cool you down? | It takes heat from the skin as it dries | It is cold water stored deep inside your body | It blocks the sun from the skin | It makes the blood flow slower
exercise: How does a dog mainly cool down? | By panting | By sweating all over | By rolling in mud | By shedding all its fur
exercise: What makes a goosebump? | A tiny muscle pulling a hair upright | A little bit of skin freezing solid in the cold | Sweat drying in a little bump | A small bruise under the skin
exercise: What makes skin darker or lighter? | How much melanin it makes | How many skin layers it has | How much sweat it makes | How much blood is near the surface
exercise: What are freckles? | Spots of extra melanin in the skin | Tiny burns from the sun | Spots of dirt that will not wash off | Small bruises that never heal
exercise: What causes sunburn? | Ultraviolet light from the sun | Only the heat you feel from the sun | Salt water drying on the skin | Sweat boiling on the skin
exercise: Which helpful vitamin does skin make in sunlight? | Vitamin D | Vitamin C | Vitamin A | Vitamin B12
exercise: Why do pigs roll in mud? | They barely sweat, so mud helps them cool down | Mud is their main food, full of worms and roots | They like to be dirty just for fun | It makes their skin grow thicker
test: «it's cloudy, don't need sun cream today» What is right? | Ultraviolet light gets through clouds, so you can still burn | Clouds block all sunburn | You only burn when it feels hot | Sun cream works only on sunny days
test: «sweat's just your body leaking, it's useless» What is the truth? | Sweat cools you as it dries off the skin | Sweat is only there to make you smell | Sweat makes you hotter | Sweat cleans out all the dirt from the blood
test: On a hot sticky humid day you sweat a lot but still feel hot. Why? | Sweat dries slowly in damp air, so it cools you less | Humid air has no oxygen in it, so the body cannot cool itself | Your sweat glands switch off | You stop sweating when it is humid
test: A cat sees a big dog and its fur stands up so it looks twice as big. What in your own skin works the same way? | Goosebumps, when tiny muscles lift your hairs | Freckles, when skin goes darker | Sweat, when the skin goes wet | Blushing, when the skin goes red
test: In a Tom and Jerry cartoon Tom gets such a fright that every hair on him shoots up stiff as pins. What is true about that? | Fur really does stand up on a scared cat, but not that stiff or all at once | Real cats can never raise their fur | Fur only stands up in cold weather | It happens to cats only in cartoons and films
test: «my mate says brown skin and white skin have totally different cells inside» What is right? | No, everyone has about the same colour cells; they just make different amounts of melanin | Yes, darker skin has extra skin layers | Yes, lighter skin has no melanin at all | No, skin colour comes from the blood
test: Count them: the skin has three main layers. A doctor looks at skin from four children. How many layers in all? | 12 | 7 | 3 | 4
test: Why do the soles of your feet need thicker skin than your eyelids? | They press on the ground with your whole weight every step | They are further from the brain, so they need extra skin to feel things | Eyelids are made of a different organ | Feet need to keep the sweat in
test: In a snowy ski resort a girl gets sunburnt on a cold day. How? | Snow reflects the sun's ultraviolet light back onto her skin | The very cold air burns the skin just like a hot flame does | Snow is full of salt | She only got cold, not burnt
test: «snake changes its skin all at once, so we must do it too, just slower innit» What is right? | We lose old skin bit by bit as tiny flakes all the time | We change our whole skin in one go, once a year in summer | We never lose any skin | We lose all our skin each night in bed
test: Why might people in a northern country take vitamin D in winter? | The winter sun is too weak for skin to make enough | Winter food has no vitamins in it | Cold kills vitamin D in the blood | Thick coats stop the skin from making enough sweat to keep it healthy
exam: Identify the organ which, of all the organs of the human body, is the largest. | The skin | The liver | The brain | The lungs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the mechanism whereby perspiration lowers body temperature? | Heat is withdrawn from the skin in the course of the evaporation of sweat | Sweat is secreted at a temperature below that of the air | Sweat obstructs the radiation of the sun | Sweat diminishes the rate of circulation of the blood
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colour of human skin. | It is determined by the quantity of melanin produced, the number of pigment cells being broadly similar in all persons | It is determined by the number of layers of which the skin is composed | It is determined by the colour of the blood | It is determined by the quantity of perspiration secreted
exam: Ascertain which of the following conditions affords no protection against sunburn. | An overcast sky | Garments covering the shoulders | A hat and shade | The application of sun cream
